<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:c="clr-namespace:EarTraining.App.Components"
             x:Class="EarTraining.App.Pages.FiveMovementsPage"
             Title="L2 C7 · The Five Movements">
    <ScrollView>
        <VerticalStackLayout Padding="20" Spacing="14">
            <Label Text="The Five Movements" FontSize="20" FontAttributes="Bold" HorizontalOptions="Center" />
            <Label Text="How a II–V–I hands off to the next key — where the key center goes, and how to hear each move."
                   HorizontalOptions="Center" TextColor="Gray" />
            <c:InfoNote Text="From Chapter 7, pp. 37–45 of the Level 2 book. Every demo starts from Dmi7–G7–Cma7 in C — the book's reference key." />

            <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}" Stroke="{AppThemeBinding Light=#512BD4, Dark=#B7A6FF}">
                <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="8">
                    <Label Text="Where the key goes" Style="{StaticResource Heading}" />
                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="10" RowSpacing="4"
                          RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto">
                        <Label Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="Movement" FontSize="12" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Text="Key center moves" FontSize="12" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2" Text="From C" FontSize="12" FontAttributes="Bold" />

                        <Label Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Text="Half-step up" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" Text="down a minor 2nd" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2" Text="C → B" FontSize="12.5" />

                        <Label Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Text="Root commontone" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1" Text="down a major 2nd" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="2" Text="C → Bb" FontSize="12.5" />

                        <Label Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="0" Text="Half-step down" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="1" Text="down a minor 3rd" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="2" Text="C → A" FontSize="12.5" />

                        <Label Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="0" Text="Circle of 4ths" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="1" Text="down a perfect 4th" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="2" Text="C → G" FontSize="12.5" />

                        <Label Grid.Row="5" Grid.Column="0" Text="Circle of 5ths" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="5" Grid.Column="1" Text="down a perfect 5th" FontSize="12.5" />
                        <Label Grid.Row="5" Grid.Column="2" Text="C → F" FontSize="12.5" />
                    </Grid>
                    <Label FontSize="13.5" Text="All five take the key center DOWN — by 1, 2, 3, 5 or 7 half-steps. They are simply the five nearest keys below the one you're leaving." />
                    <Label FontSize="13.5" Text="How far the key travels around the circle is exactly how many new accidentals you pick up — so it also tells you how chromatic the move will sound. The two circle moves are one step (one new accidental); Root commontone two; Half-step down three; Half-step up five — the most chromatic of the five." />
                </VerticalStackLayout>
            </Border>

            <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
                <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="6">
                    <Label Text="About the names" Style="{StaticResource Heading}" />
                    <Label FontSize="13.5" Text="Two of the names describe where the key goes — the circle moves. The other three describe the bass interval at the seam instead: from the root of the I chord you're leaving to the root of the II chord that follows. That's why 'Half-step up' moves the bass up a half-step but lands the key a half-step LOWER." />
                    <Label FontSize="13.5" Text="Each card below gives the key-center move first, then where the name comes from — which doubles as your fastest listening cue, since the seam is what the ear catches first." />
                </VerticalStackLayout>
            </Border>

            <VerticalStackLayout x:Name="Cards" Spacing="14" />

            <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}">
                <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="6">
                    <Label Text="Quick listening cues" Style="{StaticResource Heading}" />
                    <Label FontSize="13.5" Text="• Top notes frozen across the seam → Half-step up.&#10;• Bass frozen across the seam → Root commontone.&#10;• Seam still sounds in-key → a circle move (on Circle of 4ths the new II is diatonic to the old key).&#10;• Strong dominant pull in the bass → Circle of 5ths." />
                </VerticalStackLayout>
            </Border>

            <Label x:Name="StatusLabel" HorizontalOptions="Center" FontAttributes="Bold" />
        </VerticalStackLayout>
    </ScrollView>
</ContentPage>
